    T92
        title       Records decoded into meaning: branded identifiers, each fact stored once, full record decoding
        status      done
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@T118]

        satisfies   [@plan.Runtime.O1]
        description
            Saved records decode through Schemas built from the compiled app, with branded identifiers and each fact
            stored once, so the boot never acts on a record nobody checked.

            **Why now:** @task.V3.T20 hit the failure limit on one class: restore trusted a part of a record nobody
            checked; four reviews each found one more (a `~` name, a key naming another identity, lane keys, context
            bindings). The record Schemas in code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts decode shape only
            (`machine`, `name`, `operation`, `lane` are `Schema.String`), meaning is then checked by hand, and identity
            is stored in both key and content. T20's bindings blocker is parked here as an inverted test (@spec.Records
            R4). It must land before @task.V3.T56 (versions these records) and real storage
            (@task.V3.T58, @task.V3.T28).

            **Read first:** @task.V3.T20 (its 2026-11-18T06:49 field list and 07:05 review), @convention.boundaries.
            Spec: @spec.Records.R4 with R5, R9–R11, R14, R15, R17, R20, R21, I1–I3, I5, I8–I10, I14 of the same Spec,
            companion `wiki/specs/v3/Records.md`.

            **Build-time choice (@spec.Records companion holes H2 and H11):** (1) `graph` stores other identities'
            wiring a second time. A: derive it at a boot wake from the provider's own record where one exists, saving
            only providers with no record. B: keep `graph` as saved. A keeps each fact in one place; B is needed if a
            boot wake reads the wiring before the provider's record is admitted. Test: trace the boot wake's readers of
            `graph`; if none reads it before admission, take A and plant `graph-stored-twice`. (2) Whether today's saved
            memory holds `Flow.input` fields. Test first: save a fixture whose machine has a `Flow.input` field and read
            the record; if the field is there, step 5 to 6 removes it and the `memory` row reports nothing (as the Spec
            says), otherwise no step row is needed.

            The linked Spec is pre-coding analysis: follow it as best-guess guidance for how to build, not a strict
            contract; it should help a lot. Where building shows it wrong, fix the Spec and log the change
            in `wiki/specs/specs.log`.

            **Supersedes:** `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts#decodeRecords`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts#identityIn`, `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts#rowOf`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts#rowsRecord`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts#laneRecord`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts#keyNamesItself`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts#encodeLane`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts#encodeOwed`, `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts#rowJson`;
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record-format.ts#ActorJson`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record-format.ts#actorOf`;
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/actor-record.ts#encodeActor`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/actor-record.ts#decodeActor`;
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/world.ts#actorKey`, `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/world.ts#laneId`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/world.ts#restoredLaneId`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/world.ts#restoredAttemptId`;
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/incarnations.ts#boot`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/incarnations.ts#reportUnbound`;
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/persist.ts#laneWrite`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/persist.ts#owedWrite`,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/persist.ts#actorWrite,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/persist.ts#liveText; the boot's writes (`saveOwed`, `saveLane`,
            `incarnations.ts:446-456`). Do not delete it before its replacement lands and the suite passes.

            **Risk:** a Records Spec change, so it gets the whole-system check; the format is internal and unreleased,
            so nothing migrates.

            **Approach:** first, decide with evidence whether an actor record's context bindings can be derived from its
            machine and its ref's input; if so, stop saving them. Then:

            - branded identifiers, each made only by a Schema decode built from Compiled or by the mint (@spec.Records,
              realization: `MachineId`, `ActorName`, `StateName`, `ContractName`, `KeyText`, `AttemptId`,
              `SubjectAddress`, `ShapeStamp`); every saved name resolves through the one reference table (R15), and no
              other code looks a saved name up in a definition;
            - each fact stored once: identity only in the key (R4); a lane's or row's subject lives inside its `work`
              record (Records' lanes and rows); a headless lane stores no `queue` or `inFlight`;
            - full record decoding: one Schema per record kind (actor, lane by operation kind, owed) over those brands,
              through the walker; every hand check in `record.ts` and `actor-record.ts` after decode deleted;
            - the proof: a generated test that corrupts one field of real saved records at a time (from the fixtures'
              Stories), needing no list of fields.

            No real blocker on T81 or the boundary Task: the boot decode is internal and already uses the walker. The
            raw-throw class is parked in @task.V3.T98 (the user, 2026-11-19): a public function throwing raw on a
            hostile argument is out of scope here; a stored record decoding to a wrong ending is in scope.

            Fixtures: no catalog step (no public feature). The review findings it lands are edge values for
            @convention.fixtures: corrupt and forged saved records, drawn from every fixture's Stories, and a step
            sample that forges a read operation's lane record, which no fixture offers today.

            May change: `packages/flow-state-v3/src/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/`, @spec.Records with `specs.log` notes.
            Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: a decode that still accepts a field by hand check → caught by the corrupt-one-field test, which
            needs no list of fields. Gates: `nub run faults --only` on T20's restore faults (`record-key-unchecked`,
            `lane-key-text-unread`, `local-name-restored` and the rest) and the new `binding-unchecked`, each caught by
            its named test. User's read: whether bindings are derived or saved; any record a corrupt field leaves
            provably harmless.
        acceptance
            - Bindings: derived or saved, decided with evidence; if saved, decoded as `ActorName`s.
            - Every place a record names an actor, a lane, an Attempt or a state takes the branded type; `record.ts` has
              no `Schema.String` for an identifier and no hand meaning check after decode.
            - A record's content holds nothing its key determines.
            - The corrupt-one-field test: each corrupt record is quarantined and reported (RecordUndecodable) or shown
              harmless; the boot never fails.
            - T20's inverted Restore R5 bindings test passes; a read-operation lane record is quarantined; a headless
              lane's `queue` and `inFlight` are not read.
            - A lane record whose input or key no longer decodes is quarantined (Restore R8), and a saved state the
              machine lacks is quarantined, not restored (Restore R7); both moved from @task.V3.T56.
            - Positional actor-record frames are length-checked, moved from @task.V3.T56.
            - `StateName` and `ActorName` accept a path supplied by the machine (a hook; fragment subject paths
              are @task.V3.T109's).
            - Fixtures first (@convention.fixtures): the corrupt and forged record samples, including the read-operation
              lane, are in the fixtures' step samples and run by every runner, before the build; the proof is posted as
              a Task comment.
            - Identity and key text are parsed in one place: the six hand-parsing sites the P3 close-out named are gone.
            - Each deviation from @spec.Records is a tagged decision in a Task comment (editing the Spec is optional);
              `nub run check`, the suite and stress pass; each new runtime rule has a planted fault in `tests/faults.ts`
              that `nub run faults --only <names>` catches with its own target tests.
            - A persisted shape changes, so restore after a definition edit is shown: a reorder, an add, a remove and
              a rename.
            - Correctness: a corresponding fixture app exists that uses the feature (a whole app written the way a user
              would write it; see @convention.fixture-catalog for the apps and how to build them); a Story tests that
              fixture's feature, plus the Spec's Story of the wrong ending; a test runs the Story; type tests show the
              feature is typed fully and correctly, the way a user would expect (two-sided pins, no annotations needed
              in user code); each new runtime rule has a planted fault its own target tests catch. See the existing
              tests (`packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`, `fixtures/`) for how things are done now. Tests cover
              @spec.Records.R4 with R5, R9–R11, R14, R15, R17, R20, R21, I1–I3, I5, I8–I10, I14 of the same Spec; the
              Invariants its tests check after every step are @spec.Records.I1 with I2, I3, I5, I6, I8–I10, I14, I16,
              I18, I19 (tests T1–T3, T5–T10, T15).
