    T109
        title       Fragments: placement, copies, nesting, resumable, subject paths, subroutines and whenAbandoned 
                    (slice 3c)
        status      draft
        priority    high
        parent      [@T133]

        blocked_by  [@T57, @T113, @T119, @T131]
        satisfies   [@plan.Machines.O1, @plan.Machines.O8]
        description
            Build fragments on production: traits placed on the root or a state, `.each` copies by key, nested
            fragments, `.resumable()`, subject paths, subroutines (a fragment that is a state, with its own states and
            exits) and `whenAbandoned` (called when an incarnation ends other than by finishing).

            **Why now:** split from @task.V3.T57 by the user (2026-11-20, Q2 A). Declared emits (@task.V3.T77) address a
            fragment's emit by instance path and `.each` key, and lazy features (@task.V3.T48) load fragments. The
            subroutine and `whenAbandoned` rules moved in from @task.V3.T110 because both write one fragment
            incarnation's lifecycle (breakdown review, the orchestrator, 2026-11-20).

            **Read first:** @plan.Machines.O1, `ui-primitives-decisions` (§9; full text: git show
            3a84b878:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/2026-11-16-ui-primitives-decisions.md),
            @convention.fixture-catalog (the subroutine and `whenAbandoned` rows' fixture homes). Blocked by
            @task.V3.T57 because a trait carries schedules, which that Task finishes, and by @task.V3.T113, whose
            carried body type this Task's fragment summary adds a field to. Spec: @spec.Changes.R12 with R13, R16, I1,
            I2, I8, I10, I11 of the same Spec, companion `wiki/specs/v3/Changes.md`.

            The linked Spec is pre-coding analysis: follow it as best-guess guidance for how to build, not a strict
            contract; it should help a lot. Where building shows it wrong, fix the Spec and log the change
            in `wiki/specs/specs.log`.

            **Supersedes:** no current code of its own; the fit tables name none for this Task.

            **Contract:** this Task exposes the live copies of an `.each` trait in key order; @task.V3.T110 reads them.

            May change: `src/`, `fixtures/`, `tests/`. Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: a fragment tested only alone → caught by a fixture that places it in a host machine, with
            several `.each` copies and a reload at every step; a subroutine left only through its declared exit → caught
            by Stories that abandon it by a parent transition, a disposal and a reload.
        acceptance
            - The fragment Requirements this slice builds hold, shown by Story tests and compile-time tests, with a
              fixture that places each kind in a host machine.
            - A subroutine placed on a state runs its own states and leaves by its exits; an exit is a finish; an exit
              unreachable from its default is a `flow check` finding.
            - `whenAbandoned` runs once when an incarnation ends other than by finishing, never on a normal finish, and
              its `O.x.cancel` marks that operation's lanes for the ending incarnation's subject path.
            - `Story.fragment` exists (moved from @task.V3.T29): a Story word that runs a fragment alone.
            - `StateName` and `ActorName` (@task.V3.T92's Schemas) admit fragment subject paths (`.resumable()`), shown
              by a restore of a record that names one.
            - Each new runtime rule has a planted fault that `nub run faults --only` catches.
            - Fixtures first (@convention.fixtures): the fragment rows of step 2 of @convention.fixture-catalog, in the
              apps that row names, and the subroutine and `whenAbandoned` rows, gain their features, scenarios and
              sources; each scenario red before the build, and those cells marked used at the close.
            - `.rules(build)` on a trait and on a subroutine is typed on the real package: a trait's builder has `E` and
              `turn` but no `S`, `terminals` or `in`; a subroutine's has its own `S` and `E` with `hostEvents`; an app
              Story's rule tokens include the tokens of fragments its definitions place, shown by a two-sided type test
              at depth two (@proposal.external-rules section 1).
            - `Story.fragment(Upload, { id, runRules })` judges the trait's rules alone; a placed fragment's rules ride
              every placement and copy, and `RulesDiffer` and `flow check` walk placements (T106 owns the checks).
            - Correctness: a corresponding fixture app exists that uses the feature (a whole app written the way a user
              would write it; see @convention.fixture-catalog for the apps and how to build them); a Story tests that
              fixture's feature, plus the Spec's Story of the wrong ending; a test runs the Story; type tests show the
              feature is typed fully and correctly, the way a user would expect (two-sided pins, no annotations needed
              in user code); each new runtime rule has a planted fault its own target tests catch. See the existing
              tests (`packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`, `fixtures/`) for how things are done now. Tests cover
              @spec.Changes.R12 with R13, R16, I1, I2, I8, I10, I11 of the same Spec; the Invariants its tests check
              after every step are @spec.Changes.I1 with I2, I8, I10, I11 (tests T1, T6, T10).
