    T118
        title       Schedule issues derived from timer state, proven by sequence tests
        status      done
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@T120]
        satisfies   [@plan.Machines.O5, @plan.Machines.O8]
        description
            Schedule issues are no longer stored: each timer keeps the last outcome of each of its own checks, and the
            snapshot reads issues from those fields, so no later occasion can take or clear another's issue.

            **Why now:** four close reviews of @task.V3.T57 each found an issue or hold lost across occasions (its
            fourth: a hold restored with every pause reason removed stays held forever; an arming issue replaced by
            another check's throw, then cleared while the key stays unarmed). The second failure after convergence hit
            the failure limit; the user accepted Fable's redesign of the issue seam (2026-11-20).

            **Read first:** @spec.Schedules.R5 (the issue rule), @spec.Schedules.R13 and @spec.Schedules.R14, and its
            `algorithm` tables, @task.V3.T57 (its four close reviews),
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts. Spec: @spec.Schedules.R1 to @spec.Schedules.R17
            and @spec.Schedules.I1 to @spec.Schedules.I18, companion `wiki/specs/v3/Schedules.md`.

            The linked Spec is pre-coding analysis: follow it as best-guess guidance for how to build, not a strict
            contract; it should help a lot. Where building shows it wrong, fix the Spec and log the change
            in `wiki/specs/specs.log`.

            **Supersedes:** code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#Occasion,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#Firing,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#isScheduleSource`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#issuesAfter`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#plannedOf`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#plannedTrack`,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#ran,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#lastReasonsOf`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#checked`,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#paused,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#heldOrResumed`,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-decision.ts#decide;
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#readOf`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#contextOf`,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#pass,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#stopAll`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#unchanged`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#disarmRun`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#runningTimer`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#runningTo`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#trackedOf`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#applied`,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedules.ts#fired (its run write; the next step becomes data the
            firing's occasion carries, and @task.V3.T119 only swaps `fired` for `tick`);
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-plan.ts#savedTimersOf`,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-plan.ts#declaredOn,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-plan.ts#dueOf,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-plan.ts#dueIn;
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-restore.ts#trackedOf`,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/schedule-restore.ts#restore;
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/firings.ts#fire; `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/world.ts#Timing`,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/world.ts#Run, `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/world.ts#Tracked`,
            `packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/world.ts#withIssuesOf`;
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/host/owners/schedule-steps.ts; the schedule part of the issue read in
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/host/snapshot.ts (`scheduleIssues`, C5b of @proposal.algo-resolutions); the
            five stored-issue faults in `tests/schedule-faults.ts` (re-pointed, not deleted) and
            `tests/turn/schedule-sequences-wrong.ts` (delete once empty). Do not delete it before its replacement lands
            and the suite passes.

            **Risk:** the redesign keeps `decide`, the occasions and T57's tables; it must not reopen what they prove.
            The record format moves from 5 to 6 by the one step of @spec.Records.R12, with restore after a definition
            edit (a reason, key or schedule added, removed or renamed): this Task writes the step's schedule row
            (@spec.Schedules.R14's decode table), and @task.V3.T92 adds its other rows to the same step.

            **Approach:** the whole Task at once (the Epic's rule). First commit: the exhaustive sequence test (every
            3-step sequence on a two-key fixture, checked after each step against the ledger of owed firings from the
            Spec), red on today's code, with T57's fourth-review B1 and B2 as inverted tests titled by their rules; then
            the requirements review; then the build.

            **Build-time choice (the input gate, @spec.Schedules.Q1):** A: `pass` keeps the identities of memory,
            context, props and lanes and skips a schedule whose inputs and occasion are unchanged, never on a `Wake` or
            with a `Refused` timer. B: no gate; every scope that hears an occasion runs its reads. B is simpler and
            cannot be wrong; A saves the reads of `keys`, the reasons and `restartOn` when one copy of many changes.
            Test: add `turn.schedules.keyed-50` with a turn that touches one scope; if the reads are a measurable share
            of the turn, land A with the Level 0 check that gated and ungated runs give the same decisions, else B.

            May change: `packages/flow-state-v3/src/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/`, `tools/flow-state-lint/`, @spec.Schedules
            and @spec.Records with `specs.log` notes. Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: a sequence test whose expected results come from running the code → caught by the ledger derived
            from the Spec before any code, and a blind derivation of a sample of steps. User's read: @spec.Schedules.R5
            (an issue is a standing condition; a key that leaves or a state that exits drops its issues) and
            @spec.Schedules.R14 (a refused start marked in the record and retried on restore, format 6).
