    T119
        title       Turn parts: the walk, the applier, alarms and mail
        status      done
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@T113]
        description
            Build the turn's parts as @spec.Changes gives them (`model`, `operations`, `algorithm`, `realization`):
            `runTurn`'s walk, the one applier `apply` with rows C1 to C7, the alarm heap with `tick` and the one host
            wake, the mailbox and the index layer.

            **Why now:** fragments (@task.V3.T109), emits and the operations Tasks all write through `apply` and
            `tx.alarms`; the user placed this Task before T109.

            **Read first:** the alarm heap and `tick`, the throw family rule, emit timing and `stepEnds` are in
            @spec.Changes `model` and `algorithm`. Spec: @spec.Changes.R1 to @spec.Changes.R11, @spec.Changes.R14,
            @spec.Changes.R15, @spec.Changes.R17, @spec.Changes.R18, @spec.Changes.R21 to @spec.Changes.R26,
            @spec.Changes.I3 to @spec.Changes.I7, @spec.Changes.I9, @spec.Changes.I12 to @spec.Changes.I17,
            companion `wiki/specs/v3/Changes.md`.

            **Build-time choice (@spec.Changes.Q9, three rules no trace has tested):**

            1. The alarm kind order at one instant. A: `Bound`, `Silent`, `Release`, `Collect`, `Grace`, `Schedule`, as
               `algorithm` states (endings and releases settle before a firing reads the lanes). B: by deadline, then
               admission, kind-blind. Some total order is needed for replay either way. A is more useful if a firing at
               the same instant as a release would otherwise read a lane still `Out`. Test: a Story where a `Release`
               and a `Schedule` share an instant and the schedule's reason reads that lane; if A and B give the same
               views on every fixture, keep A as the stated total order.
            2. The command order of a step: releases before starts. A: a release frees its slot before a start on the
               same key sees it busy. B: emission order. Test: a replacing lane policy Story (one key, a running call
               replaced in one step); B shows the start waiting on the release, A does not.
            3. The wave skip when committed state and root memory are the same objects. A: skip the context wave. B:
               always run it. B cannot be wrong; A saves a wave per no-op turn. Test: Level 0 compares A against B on
               T1's closure (same views), then the bench decides whether A is worth landing.


            The linked Spec is pre-coding analysis: follow it as best-guess guidance for how to build, not a strict
            contract; it should help a lot. Where building shows it wrong, fix the Spec and log the change
            in `wiki/specs/specs.log`.

