        description
            Build the runtime half of declared schedules: the declaration object on the definition (name, `key`,
            `pausedBy`), its construction checks, and the key and selection codecs in `Compiled.schedules`.

            **Why now:** @task.V3.T118 then builds once on final shapes with one format step; the user chose this order
            (companion `Schedules.md`, the build-order table). The public types follow in @task.V3.T113 and change
            no runtime.

            **Read first:** @spec.Types.R8 (declared schedules) and its `model`; @spec.Schedules `composition`. Spec:
            @spec.Types.R8, @spec.Types.R12, @spec.Types.I11, @spec.Types.I13, @spec.Types.I14, companion
            `wiki/specs/v3/Types.md`; @spec.Schedules.R14, @spec.Schedules.R16, @spec.Schedules.I11,
            @spec.Schedules.I14, companion `wiki/specs/v3/Schedules.md`.

            The linked Spec is pre-coding analysis: follow it as best-guess guidance for how to build, not a strict
            contract; it should help a lot. Where building shows it wrong, fix the Spec and log the change
            in `wiki/specs/specs.log`.

            **Supersedes:** `ScheduleKey`, `ScheduleParts`, `KeyedConfig` and `KeyedSchedule` in `src/tokens.ts` and
            `Flow.keyed` in `src/flow.ts` (deleted by the slice's build);
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/config.ts#findingsAgainst;
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/compile-schedules.ts#scheduleOf;
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/compiled.ts#Schedule; the two metadata types in `src/tokens.ts` and
            `src/core/compiled.ts` (one `ScheduleMetadata`); the 11 `Flow.keyed` call sites in fixtures and tests are
            rewritten with it. Do not delete it before its replacement lands and the suite passes.
        acceptance
            - A schedule is declared on the definition and placed in the body (`Flow.keyed`'s removal from `src/` and
              fixtures is @task.V3.T140's).
            - `Compiled.schedules` holds each schedule's key and selection codec; a saved key decodes through it.
            - Each construction check of @spec.Types.R8 (the twin rule) has a refusing test and a planted fault.
            - Correctness: a corresponding fixture app exists that uses the feature (a whole app written the way a user
              would write it; see @convention.fixture-catalog for the apps and how to build them); a Story tests that
              fixture's feature, plus the Spec's Story of the wrong ending; a test runs the Story; type tests show the
              feature is typed fully and correctly, the way a user would expect (two-sided pins, no annotations needed
              in user code); each new runtime rule has a planted fault its own target tests catch. See the existing
              tests (`packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`, `fixtures/`) for how things are done now. Tests cover every rule
              and invariant of the Spec line above; the Invariants its tests check after every step are @spec.Types.I8,
              @spec.Types.I11, @spec.Types.I13, @spec.Types.I14 (tests T1, T2, T4, T5, T9) and @spec.Schedules.I11,
              @spec.Schedules.I14 (tests T2, T7).
